1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,477
Open the keyboard on your phone and start
a message.

2
00:00:03,797 --> 00:00:06,148
Above the keys, it suggests the next word.

3
00:00:06,468 --> 00:00:08,817
Tap the middle suggestion, again and
again.

4
00:00:09,137 --> 00:00:12,846
You get a sentence that sounds a bit like
you, and means almost nothing.

5
00:00:13,992 --> 00:00:16,938
Now take the same idea and scale it up
enormously.

6
00:00:17,258 --> 00:00:20,112
Train it on a huge share of everything
written online.

7
00:00:20,432 --> 00:00:22,030
It stops producing nonsense.

8
00:00:22,350 --> 00:00:25,459
It writes essays, answers questions and
fixes code.

9
00:00:25,779 --> 00:00:27,056
That's a language model.

10
00:00:27,376 --> 00:00:31,186
This lesson is about how it works, and why
it sometimes makes things up.

11
00:00:39,325 --> 00:00:41,008
A computer can't read words.

12
00:00:41,328 --> 00:00:42,936
It can only work with numbers.

13
00:00:43,256 --> 00:00:47,172
So the first step is to cut text into
small pieces, called tokens.

14
00:00:47,492 --> 00:00:50,104
A token can be a whole word, part of a
word,

15
00:00:50,104 --> 00:00:52,657
or a single character such as a full stop.

16
00:00:52,977 --> 00:00:57,918
Each token has a number, its ID, and the
model only ever sees those numbers.

17
00:00:59,075 --> 00:01:01,596
Common words are usually a single token.

18
00:01:01,916 --> 00:01:04,638
Longer or rarer words get split into
pieces.

19
00:01:04,958 --> 00:01:07,454
"Unbelievable" might become three tokens.

20
00:01:07,774 --> 00:01:10,089
The exact pieces depend on the model;

21
00:01:10,089 --> 00:01:14,094
each one has its own way of splitting
text, called a tokenizer.

22
00:01:15,242 --> 00:01:18,765
The full list of tokens a model knows is
its vocabulary.

23
00:01:19,085 --> 00:01:23,699
Meta's Llama 3 models, for example, have
about a hundred and twenty-eight thousand.

24
00:01:24,019 --> 00:01:28,370
A rough rule for English: a hundred tokens
is about seventy-five words.

25
00:01:29,533 --> 00:01:33,144
Other languages often need more tokens to
say the same thing.

26
00:01:33,464 --> 00:01:36,886
A 2023 study found that the same text can
take up

27
00:01:36,886 --> 00:01:41,285
to fifteen times as many tokens in one
language as in another.

28
00:01:41,604 --> 00:01:43,668
More tokens means slower answers,

29
00:01:43,668 --> 00:01:47,796
higher prices on paid services, and less
text fitting in at once.

30
00:01:48,950 --> 00:01:51,500
Tokens also explain a famous mistake.

31
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:55,114
Ask how many times the letter R appears in
"strawberry",

32
00:01:55,114 --> 00:01:57,702
and for a long time many chatbots said
two.

33
00:01:58,022 --> 00:02:01,048
The model doesn't see letters; it sees a
couple of tokens,

34
00:02:01,048 --> 00:02:02,926
something like "straw" and "berry".

35
00:02:03,246 --> 00:02:06,377
It's being asked about letters it never
directly sees.

36
00:02:10,117 --> 00:02:11,233
Now the heart of it.

37
00:02:11,553 --> 00:02:13,630
You give the model a row of tokens.

38
00:02:13,950 --> 00:02:17,608
It gives back one thing: for every token
in its vocabulary,

39
00:02:17,608 --> 00:02:20,027
how likely that token is to come next.

40
00:02:20,347 --> 00:02:22,378
After "The capital of France is",

41
00:02:22,378 --> 00:02:25,701
the token "Paris" gets almost all of the
probability.

42
00:02:26,021 --> 00:02:27,613
Most tokens get almost none.

43
00:02:28,783 --> 00:02:30,698
Then the program picks one token,

44
00:02:30,698 --> 00:02:33,773
adds it to the end, and feeds the whole
row back in.

45
00:02:34,093 --> 00:02:35,424
Predict, pick, repeat.

46
00:02:35,744 --> 00:02:38,612
One token at a time, until the answer is
finished.

47
00:02:38,932 --> 00:02:41,796
When you watch a chatbot's reply appear
piece by piece,

48
00:02:41,796 --> 00:02:43,775
that is literally what you're seeing.

49
00:02:44,908 --> 00:02:47,883
The pick isn't always the single most
likely token.

50
00:02:48,203 --> 00:02:50,204
Usually it's drawn from the top few,

51
00:02:50,204 --> 00:02:54,041
which is why the same question can get a
different answer each time.

52
00:02:54,361 --> 00:02:57,085
Lesson ten shows the setting that controls
this.

53
00:02:57,405 --> 00:02:59,875
And when a chatbot "thinks" before it
answers,

54
00:02:59,875 --> 00:03:02,721
that thinking is more tokens, produced the
same way.

55
00:03:06,450 --> 00:03:09,545
"Just predicting the next word" sounds
like a party trick.

56
00:03:09,865 --> 00:03:11,017
Here's why it isn't.

57
00:03:11,337 --> 00:03:14,545
To predict text well, you need whatever
helps you predict it.

58
00:03:14,865 --> 00:03:17,797
"She blank to the shop yesterday." That
takes grammar.

59
00:03:18,117 --> 00:03:21,003
"The Eiffel Tower is in blank." That takes
facts.

60
00:03:21,323 --> 00:03:24,652
"Twelve plus seven equals blank." That
takes arithmetic.

61
00:03:24,972 --> 00:03:28,270
"Bonjour, in English, is blank." That
takes translation.

62
00:03:29,408 --> 00:03:32,315
Now imagine the last page of a detective
novel.

63
00:03:32,635 --> 00:03:33,963
"And the murderer is blank."

64
00:03:34,283 --> 00:03:37,058
To guess that name, you'd have to follow
the whole plot.

65
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:41,339
Nobody programmed grammar or facts into
these models.

66
00:03:41,659 --> 00:03:45,393
They picked them up as a side effect of
getting better at one simple game.

67
00:03:45,713 --> 00:03:48,599
But notice what they learned: what text
tends to say.

68
00:03:48,919 --> 00:03:51,245
That's usually, but not always, what's
true.

69
00:03:54,992 --> 00:03:57,523
A model's life has two very different
phases.

70
00:03:57,843 --> 00:03:59,066
The first is training.

71
00:03:59,386 --> 00:04:01,331
The model starts as random numbers.

72
00:04:01,651 --> 00:04:04,602
It's shown a stretch of text with the next
token hidden,

73
00:04:04,602 --> 00:04:06,973
makes a guess, and is told the right
answer.

74
00:04:07,293 --> 00:04:11,434
Every number that pushed it towards the
wrong guess is nudged a tiny bit.

75
00:04:11,754 --> 00:04:15,128
Then the next stretch of text, and the
next, trillions of times.

76
00:04:15,448 --> 00:04:20,230
Meta says its Llama 3 models were trained
on more than fifteen trillion tokens.

77
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:24,842
That takes weeks or months, on thousands
of graphics chips running day and night.

78
00:04:25,992 --> 00:04:27,954
What comes out is called a base model.

79
00:04:28,274 --> 00:04:30,266
It continues text, nothing more.

80
00:04:30,586 --> 00:04:33,795
Ask it a question and it might answer with
more questions,

81
00:04:33,795 --> 00:04:35,454
like a page from a quiz book.

82
00:04:35,774 --> 00:04:37,137
So there's a second stage.

83
00:04:37,457 --> 00:04:41,283
The model is trained further on examples
of helpful conversations,

84
00:04:41,283 --> 00:04:43,602
and on people's ratings of its answers.

85
00:04:43,922 --> 00:04:46,756
That's what turns a text-continuer into an
assistant.

86
00:04:47,908 --> 00:04:51,347
The second phase is running the model,
also called inference.

87
00:04:51,667 --> 00:04:54,195
Training is over, and the numbers are
frozen.

88
00:04:54,515 --> 00:04:56,750
Your message goes in, tokens come out.

89
00:04:57,070 --> 00:04:59,682
The model does not learn from your
conversation;

90
00:04:59,682 --> 00:05:01,913
its numbers don't change while you chat.

91
00:05:02,233 --> 00:05:06,809
Whether a company keeps your chats to train its next model is a separate
question,

92
00:05:06,809 --> 00:05:08,316
and lesson four covers it.

93
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:12,937
So how does a chatbot remember what you
said three messages ago?

94
00:05:13,397 --> 00:05:14,125
It doesn't.

95
00:05:14,445 --> 00:05:18,055
The app sends the whole conversation back
in, every single time.

96
00:05:18,375 --> 00:05:22,739
The amount of text a model can take in at
once is called its context window.

97
00:05:23,059 --> 00:05:26,403
In a very long chat, the oldest parts may
no longer fit.

98
00:05:26,723 --> 00:05:28,717
And when an app says it remembers you,

99
00:05:28,717 --> 00:05:31,813
it's saving notes and adding them to the
text it sends in.

100
00:05:35,533 --> 00:05:37,429
Now lesson one's warning makes sense.

101
00:05:37,749 --> 00:05:40,669
A language model produces likely next
tokens.

102
00:05:40,989 --> 00:05:43,672
There's no built-in step that checks
whether they're true.

103
00:05:43,992 --> 00:05:47,090
When an answer appeared in its training
text many times,

104
00:05:47,090 --> 00:05:48,915
likely and true usually line up.

105
00:05:49,235 --> 00:05:53,067
When it didn't, the model still produces
the most likely-sounding text.

106
00:05:53,387 --> 00:05:54,997
A name that sounds like a name.

107
00:05:55,317 --> 00:05:56,903
A date that looks like a date.

108
00:05:57,223 --> 00:05:58,829
A source that looks like a source.

109
00:05:59,992 --> 00:06:05,149
In 2023, two lawyers in New York filed a
brief that cited six court cases.

110
00:06:05,469 --> 00:06:07,760
ChatGPT had supplied them.

111
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:09,575
None of the six existed.

112
00:06:09,895 --> 00:06:13,440
The judge fined the lawyers and their firm
five thousand dollars.

113
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:17,156
The fake cases looked real: names, quotes,
citations.

114
00:06:18,325 --> 00:06:22,489
A 2025 paper from OpenAI adds another
reason.

115
00:06:22,809 --> 00:06:26,257
The way models are trained and tested
often rewards guessing.

116
00:06:26,577 --> 00:06:30,342
Think of a multiple-choice exam where a
blank answer scores zero.

117
00:06:30,662 --> 00:06:32,043
Guessing sometimes scores,

118
00:06:32,043 --> 00:06:35,813
so a model that bluffs looks better than
one that says "I don't know".

119
00:06:36,950 --> 00:06:39,168
So the rule from lesson one gets sharper.

120
00:06:39,488 --> 00:06:43,390
The more specific, rare or recent a fact
is, the more you should check it.

121
00:06:43,710 --> 00:06:46,697
Names, numbers, dates, quotes and links
most of all.

122
00:06:47,017 --> 00:06:49,912
It helps to give the model the text you're
asking about,

123
00:06:49,912 --> 00:06:51,669
instead of relying on its memory.

124
00:06:51,989 --> 00:06:53,879
And if it gives you a source, open it.

125
00:06:57,617 --> 00:06:58,746
Let's put it together.

126
00:06:59,066 --> 00:07:02,162
Text becomes tokens, and tokens become
numbers.

127
00:07:02,482 --> 00:07:05,326
The model predicts the next token, one at
a time.

128
00:07:05,646 --> 00:07:08,425
Training sets its numbers, once, at
enormous cost.

129
00:07:08,745 --> 00:07:12,115
Running uses those numbers, and they don't
change while you chat.

130
00:07:12,435 --> 00:07:16,565
And because it predicts what's likely, not
what's true, it can be fluent and wrong.

131
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:19,121
Your task for this lesson.

132
00:07:19,441 --> 00:07:23,135
Find a question that a chatbot answered
confidently and wrongly.

133
00:07:23,455 --> 00:07:27,602
Post it in BUILD: the question, the answer, and how you found out it was
wrong.

134
00:07:27,922 --> 00:07:31,019
Any chatbot is fine, and leave out
anything personal.

135
00:07:31,339 --> 00:07:34,275
If you're stuck, ask for a source on
something specific,

136
00:07:34,275 --> 00:07:35,796
and check whether it exists.

137
00:07:36,950 --> 00:07:39,241
Next time: what a model actually is.

138
00:07:39,561 --> 00:07:42,879
A file full of numbers, and why making one
costs millions,

139
00:07:42,879 --> 00:07:47,170
while running one on your own computer
costs little more than electricity.

140
00:07:47,490 --> 00:07:48,724
See you in lesson three.
